ttywm - a window manager that looks like a tty
==============================================
Windows float in frames drawn with the console's own font, on pitch black
or a wallpaper:

	┌─[ firefox ]──────────────[x]┐
	│                             │
	└─────────────────────────────┘

The bottom line shows the network, volume, battery, date, time and which tty
you're on. Click the volume to mute, scroll on it to change it, click the
network to set up wifi.

X11 only, one C file, needs libX11, libXft, tyrmel for the terminal and launchel for the apps menu.
ttywmbg puts the wallpaper up, and works with any window manager:

	ttywmbg ~/pictures/sea.png fill
	ttywmbg black

Install
-------
	make
	sudo make install

then pick ttywm on your login screen, or put this in ~/.xinitrc and run startx:

	exec ttywm-session

Settings
--------
Everything is in ~/.config/ttywm/config, made the first time ttywm starts.
It's plain words, like

	window colour = light green
	battery = off
	super+w = firefox

Change a line and save: ttywm picks it up straight away.

New in 0.6:

	wallpaper = ~/pictures/sea.png    png or jpeg, nothing means black
	wallpaper fit = fill              fill, fit, centre or tile
	shell behind windows = on         the old look, a shell on every tty
	border typing = on            new windows type their border in first
	typing speed = fast           fast, normal or slow
	bottom line = tty clock date  what the bottom line shows, in that order
	bottom line command = uptime  your own things on it, up to 4
	window firefox = tty 2        where a program's windows go
	window mpv = full screen
	include = keys                more settings from another file
	super+h = left half           every key can do every action now

Updates
-------
When ttywm is updated, it swaps itself for the new version on its own:
your windows, shells and ttys stay as they were, no logging out.
Put "super+shift+r = restart" in your settings to do it by hand.

Keys
----
	Super+Return        terminal
	Super+d             apps menu
	Super+q             close window
	Super+Tab           next window, then the shell
	Super+f             full screen
	Super+1..9          go to tty1..tty9
	Super+Shift+1..9    send window to that tty
	Super+Shift+e       log out
	Super+drag          move
	Super+right drag    resize
	volume keys         volume

Click [x] to close, drag the title to move, drag the bottom right corner to
resize, click the shell to type in it. Exit the shell and you get a new one.
